1:1-1 (* EXPECT-ERROR: a string literal can be lent only to an RO parameter *)
2:1-15 (* This is a checked language that segfaults.  It was observed\n   2026-10-01, in the released 0.13.0, while constant tables were being\n   built.  A by-value STR parameter can be written through, a string\n   literal is read-only data, and nothing connected the two.  This\n   program passed the checker and died with SIGSEGV at the assignment,\n   exit 139, no IndexError and no message.  The same held through an RO\n   parameter lent onward to a writable one.  A count before the rule was\n   chosen found three calls in four trees that handed read-only storage\n   to a by-value slice, and none of the callees wrote.  So nothing that\n   ran had met it; the 164 procedures that DO write through a by-value\n   slice were all given real arrays.  The rule is at the call (par 2.4):\n   read-only storage goes only to an RO parameter.  A STR has no\n   subscript since it became the UTF-8 type (2026-10-31), so the\n   writable slice here is a CHAR array, and the rule still holds. *)
comments=2
